steps.body:
An index loop counts `i` from `0` up to `a.length - 1` and reads `a[i]` on each pass. Use `i < a.length`, never `<=`, or the last pass reads past the end and throws.

```java
int[] a = {4, 9, 2};
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    System.out.println(i + ": " + a[i]);
}
```

Three patterns cover most array work:

- **Sum:** start at `0` and add each element.
- **Count:** start at `0` and add 1 when an `if` matches.
- **Max or min:** start with a real candidate and replace it when you find a better one.

Start a max at `a[0]`, or at `Integer.MIN_VALUE` so any element beats it. Never start it at `0`: in an array of only negative numbers, `0` wins and is not even in the array. A min works the same way with `a[0]` or `Integer.MAX_VALUE`. Starting at `a[0]` needs at least one element, so it fails on an empty array.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {42, 77, 13, 91, 50, 68};

        int sum = 0;
        int max = values[0];
        int over50 = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            sum += values[i];
            if (values[i] > max) {
                max = values[i];
            }
            if (values[i] > 50) {
                over50++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Sum: " + sum);
        System.out.println("Max: " + max);
        System.out.println("Over 50: " + over50);
    }
}

