steps.body:
The enhanced for loop (often called for-each) hands you each element in order, with no index to manage:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int[] nums = {4, 9, 2};
        String[] names = {"Ana", "Ben", "Cai"};
        int sum = 0;
        {{code}}
        System.out.println("Sum: " + sum);
    }
}
```

```java
for (int x : nums) {
    sum += x;
}
for (String s : names) {
    System.out.println(s);
}
```

Read it as "for each `x` in `nums`". The type before the colon is the element type. It reads cleaner and cannot run off the end of the array.

The catch: the loop variable is a **copy** of the element. Assigning to it changes the copy, not the array:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int[] nums = {4, 9, 2};
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums));
    }
}
```

```java
for (int x : nums) {
    x = 0; // nums is unchanged
}
```

For a `String[]` the variable is a copy of the reference, so `s = "Zed"` leaves the array alone too.

Use the index form when you need the position `i` or when you must write into the array (`nums[i] = 0`). Use for-each whenever you only read.

example.code:
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] names = {"Ana", "Ben", "Cleo"};
        int[] scores = {72, 45, 88, 50};

        for (String name : names) {
            System.out.println("Hello, " + name);
        }

        int passing = 0;
        for (int s : scores) {
            if (s >= 50) {
                passing++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Passing: " + passing);

        for (int s : scores) {
            s = 0; // changes only the copy
        }
        System.out.println("After s = 0: " + Arrays.toString(scores));

        for (int i = 0; i < scores.length; i++) {
            scores[i] = 0;
        }
        System.out.println("After index loop: " + Arrays.toString(scores));
    }
}

