        for (int i = 0; i < temps.length; i++) {
            temps[i]++;
        }
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(temps));
    }
}

steps.body:
A plain `for` loop visits everything. When you want to stop at the first match, a `while` loop with two conditions says it directly:

```java harness
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        int[] a = {4, 9, 2};
        int target = 9;
        {{code}}
        System.out.println(i < a.length ? "Found at index " + i : "Not found");
    }
}
```

```java
int i = 0;
while (i < a.length && a[i] != target) {
    i++;
}
```

The loop ends for one of two reasons: `i` ran off the end (no match), or `a[i]` matched. Check which one afterward with `i < a.length`.

Order matters. `&&` short-circuits, so when `i < a.length` is false Java never reads `a[i]`. Swap the two tests and, when nothing matches, the last check reads `a[a.length]`, which throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`. Always put the index check first.

A `for` loop with `break` does the same job. The `while` form keeps the stopping rule in one place, and `i` is still in scope after the loop, so you can use it as the answer.

example.code:
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] readings = {4, 9, 0, -3, 7, -1};
        int[] levels = {5, 2, 8};

        int i = 0;
        while (i < readings.length && readings[i] >= 0) {
            i++;
        }
        int first = i < readings.length ? i : -1;
        System.out.println("First negative in readings: " + first);

        i = 0;
        while (i < levels.length && levels[i] >= 0) {
            i++;
        }
        first = i < levels.length ? i : -1;
        System.out.println("First negative in levels: " + first);
    }
}

